# 🧠 Technical Concepts Demonstrated

## Object-Oriented Programming

The project makes extensive use of OOP principles:

### Encapsulation

Business rules and validations are protected inside entities and services.

### Inheritance

Specialized product types inherit from a common base class:

```csharp
Product
```

Derived classes:

```csharp
ElectronicProduct
FurnitureProduct
PeripheralProduct
```

### Polymorphism

Product behavior is customized through method overriding, allowing different product types to implement specific business logic.

### Abstraction

Abstract classes are used to represent shared behaviors and characteristics among products.

---

## Generics

A generic repository was implemented:

```csharp
RepositoryService<T>
```

This allows reusable logic to be applied to different data types while maintaining type safety.

---

## Collections

The project uses:

```csharp
List<T>
Dictionary<TKey, TValue>
```

to store, organize, and process information efficiently.

---

## LINQ

LINQ was used to perform:

- Grouping operations
- Data filtering
- Aggregations
- Analytics
- Statistical processing
- Report generation
- Product ranking

---

## Repository Pattern

A generic repository layer was created to centralize data manipulation and promote code reuse.

Benefits include:

- Reusability
- Separation of concerns
- Maintainability
- Cleaner business logic

---

## Exception Handling

Custom exceptions were created to enforce business rules and improve error handling.

Examples:

```csharp
EnterpriseException
BranchException
SaleException
ProductException
CsvImportServiceException
ExcelImportServiceException
FileImportServiceException
```

---

## Interfaces and Strategy Pattern

The application uses the following interface:

```csharp
IImportService
```

which allows different import implementations:

```csharp
CsvImportService
ExcelImportService
```

This approach follows the Strategy Pattern and makes the system easier to extend.

